 <h2>Three stages of the rocket work</h2>
 <h3>1 · Building experience in RockOn!</h3><p>The Connecticut Space Grant Consortium helped make two NASA-based Wallops experiences possible for me: RockOn! first, then RockSat-C. I proved myself alongside Nick Krupa in RockOn!, where we worked with the RockOn board shown in the NASA meatball photo. That experience laid the foundation for the next stage of my rocket work. Earlier RockOn acceleration records also supported my investigation of the vibration environment; the reference plots below come from that earlier data archive.</p>
 <h3>2 · Co-leading Hartford’s first RockSat-C participation</h3><p>I went on to co-lead the University of Hartford’s first participation in RockSat-C, carrying that experience into transducer concepts, electronics, integration, procurement, budgeting, and team coordination. The second Wallops trip focused on RockSat-C payload integration and culminated in the 2025 mission’s launch, recovery, and data collection.</p>
 <h3>3 · Future research</h3><p>The next direction is to connect reviewed mission measurements with improved sensing electronics and mechanical design. My <a href="/projects/2025-08-12-pcb-demo/">simulated piezoelectric amplifier study</a> supports that future work. Harvested-energy and subsystem-performance claims will depend on the measurements.</p>
 <h2>From vibration research to payload integration</h2><p>As a RockSat-C co-lead, I worked on the vibration subsystem: analyzing accelerometer records in Python, exploring piezoelectric energy harvesting, developing the transducer assembly, and coordinating payload work, procurement, and budgets.</p>
 <p>The broader University of Hartford HAWKS mission investigated solar effects. My vibration work connected signal analysis with the design and integration of a physical experiment.</p>
 <h2>Mission outcome</h2><p>Professor Enrico Obst’s June 27, 2025 mission report confirms a successful launch, recovery, and data collection, and names Nicholas Krupa and me as the launch team. That establishes mission completion; it does not quantify the piezoelectric subsystem’s electrical output.</p>
 <h2>Measurement and logging development</h2><p>The development archive includes a four-channel piezoelectric ADC sketch and a more integrated logger combining analog channels with MPU6050 acceleration, angular-rate, temperature, and orientation fields. This work connected electrical signal acquisition with the payload’s motion record.</p><p>The small ADC test sketch reads A16, A17, A0, and A1 at 12-bit resolution, prints the raw counts, and inserts a 10 ms delay. That delay expresses a nominal acquisition pace; it is not evidence of a precisely measured 100 Hz sample rate. The code is a development artifact and has not been identified as the flown firmware.</p>
 <h2>From measurements to future research</h2><p>The January write-up frames vibration mapping as input to mechanical design, structural analysis, and later energy-harvesting experiments. The separate <a href="/projects/2025-08-12-pcb-demo/">piezoelectric amplifier study</a> explores how to condition a piezoelectric signal before digitization. Its simulation results and circuit documentation are presented on that project page.</p>
