  <div class="result-details"><div class="prose"><h3>What I extracted</h3><p>The useful engineering output was the vibration environment: X/Y/Z response, axis-to-axis behavior, dominant-frequency changes over time, amplitude changes over time, and the events where the payload went from quiet sections into stronger vibration. The IMU acceleration envelope in this source file spans X: -17.34 to 16.40 m/s², Y: -17.60 to 17.68 m/s², and Z: -24.09 to 39.50 m/s².</p><p>The separate high-range accelerometer columns also show larger Z excursions, which made the project less about one clean number and more about designing a subsystem that could compare multiple motion measurements against the piezo response.</p></div><div class="prose"><h3>How it became the 2025 experiment</h3><p>The 2025 RockSat-C code in the folder logs four analog piezo channels, A16, A17, A0, and A1, alongside MPU6050 acceleration, gyro, temperature, pitch, and roll fields. That matched my two goals: compare rocket motion against the piezo output as a vibration sensor, then evaluate whether the same vibration environment had realistic energy-harvesting potential.</p><p>Until the final 2025 piezo flight data is paired with the onboard acceleration record, this page should present the design basis and logging architecture without claiming measured harvested-power results.</p><a href="/assets/results/reference-acceleration.csv" download>Download the plotted RockOn characterization CSV ↓</a></div></div> </section>
